<article class="env-lemma" id="091D">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/091D">Lemma <span data-tag="091D">15.89.1</span>.</a> In the situation above. The functor $\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits : \textit{Mod}(\mathbf{N}, (A_ n)) \to \text{Mod}_ A$ has a right derived functor </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  R\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits : D(\textit{Mod}(\mathbf{N}, (A_ n))) \longrightarrow D(A)  \]
</div>
  <p> As usual we set $R^ p\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits (K) = H^ p(R\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits (K))$. Moreover, we have </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
for any $(M_ n)$ in $\textit{Mod}(\mathbf{N}, (A_ n))$ we have $R^ p\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits M_ n = 0$ for $p > 1$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
the object $R\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits M_ n$ of $D(\text{Mod}_ A)$ is represented by the complex 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \prod M_ n \to \prod M_ n,\quad (x_ n) \mapsto (x_ n - f_{n + 1}(x_{n + 1}))  \]
</div>
<p>
 sitting in degrees $0$ and $1$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
if $(M_ n)$ is ML, then $R^1\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits M_ n = 0$, i.e., $(M_ n)$ is right acyclic for $\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits $, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
every $K^\bullet \in D(\textit{Mod}(\mathbf{N}, (A_ n)))$ is quasi-isomorphic to a complex whose terms are right acyclic for $\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits $, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
if each $K^ p = (K^ p_ n)$ is right acyclic for $\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits $, i.e., of $R^1\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits _ n K^ p_ n = 0$, then $R\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits K$ is represented by the complex whose term in degree $p$ is $\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits _ n K_ n^ p$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The proof of this is word for word the same as the proof of Lemma <a href="/tag/07KW" data-tag="07KW">15.88.1</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>